.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt ISCSID 8
.Os macOS
.Sh NAME
.Nm iscsid
.Nd iSCSI initiator daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the iSCSI initiator.
It establishes sessions with iSCSI targets, makes the logical units of the
targets available to the system as disks, and keeps the sessions alive.
It is controlled by
.Xr iscsictl 8
and by the LunLink application, both of which start
.Nm
when it is needed.
.Pp
.Nm
runs with the privileges of the user and does not involve a kernel
extension.
A logical unit is turned into a disk in three steps: the daemon publishes
the unit as a file of a small NFS file system that it serves on a local
socket; that file system is mounted with the NFS client of the system; and
the file is attached as a raw disk image.
From there on the system treats the unit like any other disk.
.Pp
When the connection of a session is lost,
.Nm
suspends I/O to the disks of the session and attempts to re-establish the
session.
If that succeeds within two minutes, I/O resumes where it left off.
Otherwise the disks are detached, and targets that are configured to be
persistent or to be logged in to automatically are logged in to again once
they can be reached.
.Pp
Upon the signals
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGINT
.Nm
unmounts the volumes of all targets (forcibly if need be), logs out and
exits.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Log debug messages.
.It Fl v
Display the version and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev ISCSI_RUNTIME_DIR
The directory for run-time files; the log is kept there as well.
.It Ev ISCSI_PREFERENCES_DOMAIN
The preferences domain that holds the configuration.
.It Ev ISCSI_SECRETS_FILE
A property list file in which to keep CHAP secrets, instead of the keychain.
.It Ev ISCSI_RECOVERY_TIMEOUT
The time, in seconds, for which to attempt to recover a session before its
disks are detached (120 by default).
.El
.Sh FILES
See
.Xr iscsictl 8 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr iscsictl 8 ,
.Xr launchd 8 ,
.Xr mount_nfs 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
implements the iSCSI protocol (RFC 7143) at error recovery level 0, with a
single connection per session.
CHAP authentication uses the MD5 algorithm.
